diff --git a/docs/business-rules.md b/docs/business-rules.md
index d72c8c7..16e3fd7 100644
--- a/docs/business-rules.md
+++ b/docs/business-rules.md
@@ -98,7 +98,19 @@ ### Ticket
 
 **Immutability enforcement:** the write-once rule is enforced on the `Ticket` model (`updating` hook), so it applies to Eloquent saves only — mass updates through the query builder (`Ticket::query()->update(...)`) bypass it and must not be used on issued tickets.
 
-**Single-ticket call (scope note):** `Ticket::call()` marks one waiting ticket as `called` (entity-membership checked, exactly-once). This minimal domain primitive proves the mutable `status`/`called_*` fields. Deterministic call-next selection (priority + age + 3-ticket fairness) is implemented by `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` with the three call-panel modes below (`any` applies fairness; `priority_only`/`normal_only` ignore it); AuthZ stays out of scope per #20 and HTTP/Livewire panels remain future work.
+**Single-ticket call:** `Ticket::call()` locks the ticket and its queue, rejects inactive queues (`InactiveQueueException`), authorizes the caller through the `Queue` policy (`AuthorizationException`, even when the ticket was already called), then marks one waiting ticket as `called` exactly once (`LogicException` for non-waiting tickets). Deterministic call-next selection (priority + age + 3-ticket fairness) is implemented by `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` with the three call-panel modes below (`any` applies fairness; `priority_only`/`normal_only` ignore it); after locking the queue and checking it is active, it authorizes the caller (`AuthorizationException`, no ticket changes) in every call mode. HTTP/Livewire panels remain future work.
+
+### Queue Lifecycle and Per-Queue Access
+
+Queues are managed by owner/admin of the entity only (domain actions `App\Actions\CreateQueue` / `App\Actions\UpdateQueue`, enforced through the `Queue` policy; employees, members of other entities and non-members get `AuthorizationException` and nothing is written):
+
+- **Create:** name is trimmed (1–255 chars) with slug defaulting to `Str::slug(name)` (explicit slugs are slugified too); new queues are active. Slugs are unique within an entity (the same slug may exist in another entity); duplicates are a `ValidationException`, including races on the DB unique index.
+- **Update:** any combination of new name, new slug and active flag (`null` means leave unchanged); keeping the queue's own slug is fine, taking another queue's slug is a `ValidationException`.
+- **No hard delete:** deactivation (`is_active = false`) retires a queue — its tickets are preserved, and issuing or calling on it keeps failing with `InactiveQueueException`.
+
+**Who may call:** the `Queue` policy (`App\Policies\QueuePolicy`) is the single source of truth — `create` (owner/admin of the entity), `update`/`manageAccess` (owner/admin of the queue's entity), and `call` (owner/admin always, no grant needed; an employee only with a `queue_access` row for that queue whose `permissions` array contains `call`; everyone else denied).
+
+**Access grants:** `queue_access` rows (`entity_membership_id`, `queue_id`, `permissions` JSON array; unique per pair) hold per-queue employee permissions — today only `call` (`App\Enums\QueuePermission`). A manager sets a membership's permissions via `App\Actions\SetQueueAccess`: a non-empty list upserts the row, an empty list revokes (deletes) it; unknown/non-string entries and cross-entity membership/queue pairs are a `ValidationException` with nothing stored. Grants log `queue.access.granted` and revokes log `queue.access.revoked` on the `audit` channel (context: `actor_id`, `queue_id`, `entity_membership_id`); no log when there was no row to revoke.
 
 ### Priority
 
